explain.narrative:
## Core concept
RIGHT JOIN preserves every row from the right input and attaches matching rows from the left input. When no left-side row matches, left-side columns become NULL. The OUTER keyword is optional in RIGHT OUTER JOIN.

## SQLite syntax
```sql
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS orders;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS customers;
SELECT a.columns, b.columns\nFROM table_a AS a\nRIGHT JOIN table_b AS b ON a.key_column = b.key_column;
```

## Sample schema and data
The examples use a small customers table and an orders table. customer_id is the relationship key. The sample deliberately includes two customers without orders and one order whose customer_id has no matching customer. This lets you observe both matching and unmatched rows.

## Worked examples
### 1. Create and inspect the sample data
```sql
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS orders;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS customers;
CREATE TABLE customers (customer_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, customer_name TEXT NOT NULL, city TEXT);
CREATE TABLE orders (order_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, customer_id INTEGER, order_total NUMERIC NOT NULL);
INSERT INTO customers VALUES (1,'Asha Rao','Rajahmundry'),(2,'Bilal Khan','Hyderabad'),(3,'Chitra Das','Vijayawada'),(4,'Dev Patel','Mumbai');
INSERT INTO orders VALUES (101,1,120),(102,1,80),(103,3,50),(104,9,40);
SELECT COUNT(*) AS order_count FROM orders;
```
Expected result: 4

The right input will be orders, so all four order rows must appear in a RIGHT JOIN result.

### 2. Preserve every order
```sql
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS orders;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS customers;
CREATE TABLE customers (customer_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, customer_name TEXT NOT NULL, city TEXT);
CREATE TABLE orders (order_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, customer_id INTEGER, order_total NUMERIC NOT NULL);
INSERT INTO customers VALUES (1,'Asha Rao','Rajahmundry'),(2,'Bilal Khan','Hyderabad'),(3,'Chitra Das','Vijayawada'),(4,'Dev Patel','Mumbai');
INSERT INTO orders VALUES (101,1,120),(102,1,80),(103,3,50),(104,9,40);
SELECT o.order_id, c.customer_name FROM customers AS c RIGHT JOIN orders AS o ON c.customer_id = o.customer_id ORDER BY o.order_id;
```
Expected result: 101 | Asha Rao; 102 | Asha Rao; 103 | Chitra Das; 104 | NULL

Order 104 is retained even though customer_id 9 has no matching customer.

### 3. Identify orders without a customer match
```sql
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS orders;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS customers;
CREATE TABLE customers (customer_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, customer_name TEXT NOT NULL, city TEXT);
CREATE TABLE orders (order_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, customer_id INTEGER, order_total NUMERIC NOT NULL);
INSERT INTO customers VALUES (1,'Asha Rao','Rajahmundry'),(2,'Bilal Khan','Hyderabad'),(3,'Chitra Das','Vijayawada'),(4,'Dev Patel','Mumbai');
INSERT INTO orders VALUES (101,1,120),(102,1,80),(103,3,50),(104,9,40);
SELECT o.order_id, o.customer_id FROM customers AS c RIGHT JOIN orders AS o ON c.customer_id = o.customer_id WHERE c.customer_id IS NULL ORDER BY o.order_id;
```
Expected result: 104 | 9

A NULL value in the non-nullable customer primary key indicates that no customer row matched.

### 4. Express the same preservation with LEFT JOIN
```sql
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS orders;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS customers;
CREATE TABLE customers (customer_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, customer_name TEXT NOT NULL, city TEXT);
CREATE TABLE orders (order_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, customer_id INTEGER, order_total NUMERIC NOT NULL);
INSERT INTO customers VALUES (1,'Asha Rao','Rajahmundry'),(2,'Bilal Khan','Hyderabad'),(3,'Chitra Das','Vijayawada'),(4,'Dev Patel','Mumbai');
INSERT INTO orders VALUES (101,1,120),(102,1,80),(103,3,50),(104,9,40);
SELECT o.order_id, c.customer_name FROM orders AS o LEFT JOIN customers AS c ON c.customer_id = o.customer_id ORDER BY o.order_id;
```
Expected result: 101 | Asha Rao; 102 | Asha Rao; 103 | Chitra Das; 104 | NULL

Swapping the table order and using LEFT JOIN preserves the same order population and is supported by older SQLite versions.

### 5. Filter the right-side population without dropping unmatched orders
```sql
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS orders;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS customers;
CREATE TABLE customers (customer_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, customer_name TEXT NOT NULL, city TEXT);
CREATE TABLE orders (order_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, customer_id INTEGER, order_total NUMERIC NOT NULL);
INSERT INTO customers VALUES (1,'Asha Rao','Rajahmundry'),(2,'Bilal Khan','Hyderabad'),(3,'Chitra Das','Vijayawada'),(4,'Dev Patel','Mumbai');
INSERT INTO orders VALUES (101,1,120),(102,1,80),(103,3,50),(104,9,40);
SELECT o.order_id, c.customer_name FROM customers AS c RIGHT JOIN orders AS o ON c.customer_id = o.customer_id AND o.order_total >= 80 ORDER BY o.order_id;
```
Expected result: 101 | Asha Rao; 102 | Asha Rao; 103 | NULL; 104 | NULL

The ON condition defines qualifying matches while the RIGHT JOIN still preserves every order.

## Query behaviour, NULLs and dialect notes
The examples target SQLite. A join predicate that evaluates to TRUE forms a matching pair; FALSE or UNKNOWN does not. Outer joins create NULL-extended columns for missing partners. Use a non-nullable key from the missing side when detecting unmatched rows. Do not assume RIGHT or FULL OUTER JOIN exists in every engine version: SQLite supports them from 3.39.0; older SQLite releases can express a RIGHT JOIN by reversing inputs and using LEFT JOIN. For older engines without FULL OUTER JOIN, a carefully constructed UNION ALL of a left join and right-only rows may be required. Always verify the target DBMS documentation.

## Common mistakes
- Joining on the wrong columns or omitting part of a composite relationship.
- Forgetting that one-to-many matches produce multiple output rows.
- Using SELECT * in a report with repeated column names.
- Filtering a nullable right-side column in WHERE and unintentionally removing unmatched rows from a LEFT JOIN.
- Counting rows with COUNT(*) when COUNT(right_table.primary_key) is needed to count matches.

## Best practices
Use explicit JOIN syntax, meaningful aliases, qualified column names and a deliberate ON condition. Test with matched, unmatched and duplicate-match data. Add ORDER BY when output order matters; SQL does not guarantee row order without it.

